"Univ.-Prof. Dr. Kevin Tang (https://www.kevintang.org/Files/CV/Tang_Kevin_CV.pdf)\nciteturn31905search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Proceeding articles (published or accepted) ... Character-aware transformers learn an irregular morphological pattern yet ... Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EACL 2026, 1431–\n2015\nAndrew Nevins, Cilene Rodrigues & Kevin Tang. 2015. The rise and fall of\nthe L-shaped morphome: diachronic and experimental studies. Probus: Inter-\nnational Journal of Latin and Romance Linguistics 27(1). (Equal contribution\nauthorship: Nevins, Rodrigues and Tang), 101–155. http://dx.doi.org/10.\n1515/probus-2015-0002 [SSCI, A&HCI] [Ƃ pdf | Ĩ bib]\nProceeding articles (published or accepted)\nAccepted\nAkhilesh Kakolu Ramarao, Kevin Tang & Dinah Baer-Henney.\n2026.\nCharacter-aware transformers learn an irregular morphological pattern yet\nnone generalize like humans. In Byung-Doh Oh, Tatsuki Kuribayashi, Giulia\nRambelli, Ece Takmaz, Philipp Wicke, Jixing Li & Ryo Yoshida (eds.), Pro-\nceedings of the 15th workshop on cognitive modeling and computational linguis-\ntics. Accepted (Preprint: https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.14100).\nPalma, Mallorca, Spain: Association for Computational Linguistics. Forth-\ncoming [Student Author: AKR]\nAccepted\nRayyan Merchant, Akhilesh Kakolu Ramarao & Kevin Tang. Accepted. Con-\nnecting the Persian-speaking world through transliteration. In Advances in\nIranian linguistics III. Preprint: https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2502.\n20047. John Benjamins [Student Authors: RM, AKR; Senior Author: KT]\n[Ƈ data | Ƈ code]\n2026\nRayyan Merchant & Kevin Tang. 2026a. ParsTranslit: truly versatile Tajik-\nFarsi transliteration. In Vera Demberg, Kentaro Inui & Lluís Marquez (eds.),\nFindings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EACL 2026, 1431–\n1443.\nRabat, Morocco: Association for Computational Linguistics.\nhttp:\n/ / dx . doi . org / 10 . 18653 / v1 / 2026 . findings - eacl . 73.\nhttps : / /\naclanthology.org/2026.findings-eacl.73/\n[Student Author: RM] [Ƃ\npdf | Ĩ bib | Ƈ data and code]\n9--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacter-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans (https://www.emergentmind.com/papers/2602.14100)\nciteturn31905search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; # Character-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like HumansPublished 15 Feb 2026 in cs.CL | (2602.14100v1)\n\n# Character-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans\n\nPublished 15 Feb 2026 in cs.CL | (2602.14100v1)\n\nAbstract: Whether neural networks can serve as cognitive models of morphological learning remains an open question. Recent work has shown that encoder-decoder models can acquire irregular patterns, but evidence that they generalize these patterns like humans is mixed. We investigate this using the Spanish \\emph{L-shaped morphome}, where only the first-person singular indicative (e.g., \\textit{pongo} `I put') shares its stem with all subjunctive forms (e.g., \\textit{ponga, pongas}) despite lacking apparent phonological, semantic, or syntactic motivation. We compare five encoder-decoder transformers varying along two dimensions: sequential vs. position-invariant positional encoding, and atomic vs. decomposed tag representations. Positional encoding proves decisive: position-invariant models recover the correct L-shaped paradigm clustering even when L-shaped verbs are scarce in training, whereas sequential positional encoding models only partially capture the pattern. Yet none of the models productively generalize this pattern to novel forms. Position-invariant models generalize the L-shaped stem across subjunctive cells but fail to extend it to the first-person singular indicative, producing a mood-based generalization rather than the L-shaped morphomic pattern. Humans do the opposite, generalizing preferentially to the first-person singular indicative over subjunctive forms. None of the models reproduce the human pattern, highlighting the gap between statistical pattern reproduction and morphological abstraction.\n\nAuthors (3)\n\n  1. Akhilesh Kakolu Ramarao \n  2. Kevin Tang \n  3. Dinah Baer-Henney --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLLM_safety_papers/ICML_2026.md at main · zxzhan/LLM_safety_papers · GitHub (https://github.com/zxzhan/LLM_safety_papers/blob/main/ICML_2026.md)\nciteturn31905search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; ### Mitigating the Safety–Utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning ... It proposes Adaptive Safe Context Learning (ASCL), which models safety alignment as multi-turn tool calling, letting the model autonomously decide when to consult safety rules and how to continue reasoning, and introduces Inverse Frequency Policy Optimization (IFPO) to rebalance advantage estimation.\n\n# ICML_2026.md\n\n## File metadata and controls\n\n  * Preview\n\n  * Code\n\n  * Blame\n\n730 lines (535 loc) · 106 KB\n\nRaw\n\nCopy raw file\n\nDownload raw file\n\nOutline\n\nEdit and raw actions\n\n# ICML 2026 — LLM Safety Papers\n\n> Source: Accepted paper list on the official ICML 2026 Virtual site Conference: The 43rd ICML, July 6–11, 2026, Seoul, South Korea Scope: Out of all 6,634 accepted papers, keyword filtering yielded 156 core LLM-safety papers\n\n## Notes\n\n  * Each entry's title comes from the official ICML Virtual site; the Link is the paper's ICML poster page (which contains the official abstract, authors, and PDF/OpenReview entry points); the Summary is a Chinese summary based on the actual abstract on the poster page.\n  * The scope is core LLM safety (per the requirements, the following have been excluded: AI text watermarking, machine unlearning, hallucination detection and sycophancy, as well as non-LLM Safe RL/robotic control, time-series/graph/federated-learning backdoors, VLM adversarial-robustness defenses, purely cybersecurity-capability benchmarks, etc.). ## 2. Safety Alignment: Training, Mechanism, and Representation\n\n### Mitigating the Safety–Utility Trade-off in LLM Alignment via Adaptive Safe Context Learning\n\n  * Link: https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/63381\n  * Summary: Points out that existing methods that build safety-rule chains-of-thought via context distillation create a rigid association between rule memorization and refusal, thereby constraining reasoning. It proposes Adaptive Safe Context Learning (ASCL), which models safety alignment as multi-turn tool calling, letting the model autonomously decide when to consult safety rules and how to continue reasoning, and introduces Inverse Frequency Policy Optimization (IFPO) to rebalance advantage estimation.\n\n### MESA: Improving MoE Safety Alignment via Decentralized Expertise\n\n  * Link: https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/63939\n  * Summary: Points out that Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) suffers from a \"safety sparsity\" vulnerability (safety capability concentrated in a few experts is easily bypassed). \n### Controlling the Risk of Corrupted Contexts for Language Models via Early-Exiting\n\n  * Link: https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/65947\n  * Summary: Using the model's no-context (zero-shot) performance as a \"safe\" baseline, it applies Distribution-Free Risk Control (DFRC) to prevent user-provided context from dropping performance below that baseline. It uses dynamic early-exiting to predict and ignore the later attention heads that attend most to unsafe input, and improves DFRC to balance performance and efficiency on beneficial inputs, validated on 9 in-context-learning and open-ended QA tasks.\n\n### ``Someone Hid It!'': Query-Agnostic Black-Box Attacks on LLM-Based Retrieval\n\n  * Link: https://icml.cc/virtual/2026/poster/62909\n  * Summary: Proposes query-agnostic black-box attacks on LLM-based retrieval systems, using a zero-shot surrogate LLM to generate transferable injection tokens without knowledge of the victim's query or victim model. It models the attack as an optimization problem, finding optimal adversarial tokens via adversarial learning with learnable query samples, and validates the vulnerability of RAG, dense retrieval, and agent memory across various retrievers.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCharacter-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans | AI Research Paper Details (https://www.aimodels.fyi/papers/arxiv/character-aware-transformers-learn-irregular-morphological-pattern)\nciteturn31905search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; # Character-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like HumansWhy can't character-aware transformers learn this simple Spanish verb trick that *every* human masters?Published 2/17/2026 by Akhilesh Kakolu Ramarao, Kevin Tang, Dinah Baer-Henney\n\n# Character-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans\n\nWhy can't character-aware transformers learn this simple Spanish verb trick that *every* human masters?\n\nPublished 2/17/2026 by Akhilesh Kakolu Ramarao, Kevin Tang, Dinah Baer-Henney\n\n\n## Overview\n\n  * Transformer models that process text character-by-character can learn irregular patterns in word formation\n  * The models successfully capture how languages handle words that don't follow standard rules\n  * Despite this success, these AI systems fail to generalize in the same way humans do\n  * Human learners can apply learned patterns to new situations more flexibly than current models\n  * The research tests models on Spanish verb conjugations, which contain many irregular forms\n\n## Plain English Explanation\n\nLanguage has rules, but those rules have exceptions. When you learn English, you know that regular verbs add \"ed\" to make the past tense: walk becomes walked. But irregular verbs break this pattern: go becomes went, not goed. Kids learn these patterns and can handle new words they've never seen before. If you invented a new verb like \"to blit,\" most English speakers would correctly guess \"I blit, I blitted.\"\n\nThis paper examines whether transformer models—the kind of AI that powers modern language models—can learn irregular patterns the way humans do. The researchers focused on Spanish verbs, which have many irregular forms that don't follow the standard conjugation patterns.\n\nThe surprising finding is that these models can learn the irregular patterns. When you show them enough examples of how certain verbs break the rules, they pick up on it. But here's where humans win: when the model encounters new situations, it doesn't generalize as flexibly as a person would. A human learns the underlying principle behind an irregularity and applies it creatively. The model learns the pattern more mechanically, like memorizing a list rather than understanding a principle.\n\n## Key Findings\n\n  * Character-aware transformer models successfully learn irregular morphological patterns in Spanish verb conjugation\n  * The models achieve this learning with relatively little data about the irregular verbs\n  * However, the models generalize to new words less effectively than human speakers do\n  * No tested model architecture generalizes in a human-like manner to unseen words\n  * The models appear to rely on surface-level pattern matching rather than deeper linguistic principles\n  * Performance on irregular patterns remains significantly below human-level generalization across all tested approaches\n\n## Technical Explanation\n\nThe researchers built an experiment using transformer models that process Spanish verbs at the character level rather than treating whole words as units. This approach allows the models to learn letter-by-letter transformations, which is relevant for understanding how morphological changes work.\n\nThe key architectural choice involves character-aware processing, where the model sees Spanish verbs as sequences of individual letters rather than pre-packaged units. This design choice mirrors how humans might reconstruct morphological patterns—by noticing what letters change between different forms of the same verb.\n\nThe experiments involved training models on Spanish verb conjugations with varying amounts of irregular verb examples. The researchers then tested whether models could conjugate verbs they had never encountered before. This tests generalization: can the model apply what it learned about irregularity to genuinely new cases?\n\nThe methodology compared multiple model variants and training regimes. Different architectural choices produced similar results, all falling short of human performance. The consistency of this finding across different model configurations suggests the limitation stems from something fundamental about how these models learn, rather than from specific technical choices.\n\nThe implications matter for understanding how language models learn morphological structure. Current models can memorize patterns but struggle with the kind of abstraction humans naturally perform. This reveals an important gap between machine learning and human cognition—one that remains unresolved despite the impressive performance of modern AI systems on many language tasks.\nOr do they learn something entirely different that happens to work on training data but fails on novel cases? This distinction matters for understanding what's happening inside the model.\n\nThere's also a question about evaluation. The paper measures generalization but doesn't deeply explore partial success. Some errors are closer to correct than others. A human might over-regularize occasionally, making the same mistakes a model makes. The paper could benefit from more nuanced error analysis rather than binary correct/incorrect judgments.\n\nFinally, the research benchmarks against human performance but doesn't explore the cognitive mechanisms of human generalization deeply. What exactly allows humans to generalize? Is it simply more experience, or do humans use fundamentally different learning mechanisms? Without clearer understanding of the human baseline, it's hard to interpret how large the gap really is.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe research demonstrates that transformer models can capture irregular patterns in language but reveals a ceiling on their ability to generalize in human-like ways. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKevin TANG | University Professor | PhD (UCL), MEng (Hons), MA (Cantab & UCL), CMALT | Heinrich Heine University Düsseldorf, Düsseldorf | HHU | Department of English and American Studies | Research profile (https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Kevin-Tang-7)\nciteturn31905search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; This lack of data has made it difficult to research language in African American communities and has been shown to cause emerging technologies such as Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) to perform worse for Afri... ...   * Aug 2026 ... Character-aware Transformers Learn an Irregular Morphological Pattern Yet None Generalize Like Humans ... Equinox Publishing: Sheffield, UK.\nThis lack of data has made it difficult to research language in African American communities and has been shown to cause emerging technologies such as Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) to perform worse for Afri...\n\nView\n\nProbing Character-level Transformers for the Spanish L-shaped Morphome\n\nPreprint\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Aug 2026\n\n  * Akhilesh Kakolu Ramarao\n  * Kevin Tang\n  * Wiebke Petersen\n  * Dinah Baer-Henney\n\nWhen a transformer learns an irregular morphological pattern, what has it learned? Our test case is the Spanish \\emph{L-shaped morphome}, a comple